ntinue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"5ae748dbc6bb2d90733975b5d3bf52867964e07899779c07d5a4117ffcd2bc8f"},"seconds":10.181572437286377,"seed":95974694886429739,"stop":"eos","text":"Therefore, we need to consider the squares of integers from 4 to 9. We calculate these squares to identify which have 6 as their units digit:\n\n- \\(4^2 = 16\\)\n- \\(5^2 = 25\\)\n- \\(6^2 = 36\\)\n- \\(7^2 = 49\\)\n- \\(8^2 = 64\\)\n- \\(9^2 = 81\\)\n\nFrom the above, the numbers with 6 as the units digit are 16, 36, and 64. The tens digits \\(B\\) are 1, 3, and 6, respectively.\n\nThus, there are 3 distinct possible values for \\(B\\).\n\n\\boxed{3}"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{3}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"Therefore, we need to consider the squares of integers from 4 to 9. We calculate these squares to identify which have 6 as their units digit:\n\n- \\(4^2 = 16\\)\n- \\(5^2 = 25\\)\n- \\(6^2 = 36\\)\n- \\(7^2 = 49\\)\n- \\(8^2 = 64\\)\n- \\(9^2 = 81\\)\n\nFrom the above, the numbers with 6 as the units digit are 16, 36, and 64. The tens digits \\(B\\) are 1, 3, and 6, respectively.\n\nThus, there are 3 distinct possible values for \\(B\\).\n\n\\boxed{3}","id":"number_theory:183"}
